116	The morph works without any CSS. To customize it, add `share="morph"` together with `default="none"`. The `share` prop assigns the `morph` class to the view transition, which you can target with CSS pseudo-elements. For example, to soften the morph mid-flight with a [`blur`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/filter-function/blur) keyframe:
117	
118	```tsx
119	<ViewTransition name={`photo-${photo.id}`} share="morph" default="none">
120	  <Image src={photo.src} alt={photo.title} />
121	</ViewTransition>
122	```
123	
124	Add the same props to both Step 1 snippets (`photo-grid.tsx` and `photo-content.tsx`). `default="none"` keeps each named image from running its own crossfade on every unrelated transition. Without it, every named `<ViewTransition>` animates whenever any transition runs on the page. When you add `default="none"` to a named pair, keep the explicit `share`. With `default="none"` and no `share` prop, the pair silently stops morphing.
125	
126	```css filename="app/globals.css"
127	::view-transition-group(.morph) {
128	  animation-duration: 400ms;
129	}
130	::view-transition-image-pair(.morph) {
131	  animation-name: via-blur;
132	}
133	@keyframes via-blur {
134	  30% {
135	    filter: blur(3px);
136	  }
137	}
138	```
139	
140	The blur hides pixel-level interpolation artifacts during the transition. At 400ms, the morph is slow enough to register but fast enough to feel direct.
141	
142	### Step 2: Animate loading states with Suspense reveals
143	
144	The photo detail page loads its content asynchronously. While data is in flight, a Suspense boundary shows a skeleton. When the data resolves, the skeleton is replaced by the real content.
145	
146	Without a transition, the swap is instant. The skeleton vanishes and the content pops in.
147	
148	In motion design, vertical direction encodes hierarchy. Content sliding up communicates arrival. Content sliding down communicates departure. The pair together creates a handoff: the placeholder yields to the real thing.
149	
150	Wrap the Suspense fallback in a `ViewTransition` with an exit animation, and the content in a `ViewTransition` with an enter animation:
151	
152	```tsx filename="app/photo/[id]/page.tsx"
153	import { Suspense, ViewTransition } from 'react'
154	
155	export default async function PhotoPage({ params }) {
156	  const { id } = await params
157	
158	  return (
159	    <Suspense
160	      fallback={
161	        <ViewTransition exit="slide-down" default="none">
162	          <PhotoContentSkeleton />
163	        </ViewTransition>
164	      }
165	    >
166	      <ViewTransition enter="slide-up" default="none">
167	        <PhotoContent id={id} />
168	      </ViewTransition>
169	    </Suspense>
170	  )
171	}
172	```
173	
174	As in Step 1, `default="none"` prevents this `ViewTransition` from animating during unrelated transitions, like the shared element morph.
175	
176	The CSS animations use asymmetric timing. The exit is fast (150ms). The enter fade is slower (210ms) and delayed until the exit completes, while its slide movement runs longer (400ms):
177	
178	```css filename="app/globals.css"
179	:root {
180	  --duration-exit: 150ms;
181	  --duration-enter: 210ms;
182	  --duration-move: 400ms;
183	}
184	
185	::view-transition-old(.slide-down) {
186	  animation:
187	    var(--duration-exit) ease-out both fade reverse,
188	    var(--duration-exit) ease-out both slide-y reverse;
189	}
190	::view-transition-new(.slide-up) {
191	  animation:
192	    var(--duration-enter) ease-in var(--duration-exit) both fade,
193	    var(--duration-move) ease-in both slide-y;
194	}
195	
196	@keyframes fade {
197	  from {
198	    filter: blur(3px);
199	    opacity: 0;
200	  }
201	  to {
202	    filter: blur(0);
203	    opacity: 1;
204	  }
205	}
206	@keyframes slide-y {
207	  from {
208	    transform: translateY(10px);
209	  }
210	  to {
211	    transform: translateY(0);
212	  }
213	}
214	```
215	
216	The asymmetry is deliberate. Old content should leave quickly so it does not compete for attention. New content should arrive more gently so the user has time to register it. The `var(--duration-exit)` delay on the enter fade means the new content waits for the old content to finish leaving before it becomes visible.
217	
218	If we refresh the page, the skeleton slides down and fades out, and a moment later the real content slides up and fades in.
219	
220	### Step 3: Add directional motion for navigation
221	
222	The gallery now has morphing images and animated loading states. But navigating between pages still has no directional signal. Forward and back navigations look identical. The user cannot tell from the animation whether they moved deeper into the app or returned to a previous page.
223	
224	In film and animation, horizontal direction encodes spatial position. Moving left means progressing forward (like turning a page in a left-to-right language). Moving right means going back. This convention is so ingrained that violating it feels disorienting.
225	
226	Use the `transitionTypes` prop on `<Link>` to tag forward navigations:
227	
228	```tsx filename="components/photo-grid.tsx"
229	<Link href={`/photo/${photo.id}`} transitionTypes={['nav-forward']}>
230	  {/* photo thumbnail */}
231	</Link>
232	```
233	
234	The same pattern works for any navigation within the app. For example, previous/next arrows on a photo detail page can use `nav-back` and `nav-forward` to animate in the corresponding direction.
235	
236	For links that return the user to a previous page, use `nav-back`:
237	
238	```tsx filename="app/photo/[id]/page.tsx"
239	<Link href="/" transitionTypes={['nav-back']}>
240	  ← Gallery
241	</Link>
242	```
243	
244	The transition type is not automatic. You decide which links are "forward" and which are "back" based on your app's navigation hierarchy.
245	
246	Then wrap page content in a `ViewTransition` that maps transition types to directional animations:
247	
248	```tsx filename="app/photo/[id]/page.tsx"
249	<ViewTransition
250	  enter={{
251	    'nav-forward': 'nav-forward',
252	    'nav-back': 'nav-back',
253	    default: 'none',
254	  }}
255	  exit={{
256	    'nav-forward': 'nav-forward',
257	    'nav-back': 'nav-back',
258	    default: 'none',
259	  }}
260	  default="none"
261	>
262	  {/* page content */}
263	</ViewTransition>
264	```
265	
266	The `enter` and `exit` props accept an object keyed by transition type. When a navigation carries the `nav-forward` type, the exit animation slides old content left and the enter animation slides new content in from the right. The `default: "none"` ensures that transitions without a type (browser back/forward, `router.refresh()`, Suspense reveals) produce no directional animation.
267	
268	Wrap every participating page the same way. For the gallery to slide out on forward navigation and back in on `nav-back`, its page needs the same wrapper. Put the wrapper in each `page.tsx`, not the layout. Layouts persist across navigations, so enter and exit never fire there:
269	
270	```tsx filename="app/page.tsx"
271	<ViewTransition
272	  enter={{
273	    'nav-forward': 'nav-forward',
274	    'nav-back': 'nav-back',
275	    default: 'none',
276	  }}
277	  exit={{
278	    'nav-forward': 'nav-forward',
279	    'nav-back': 'nav-back',
280	    default: 'none',
281	  }}
282	  default="none"
283	>
284	  <PhotoGrid photos={photos} />
285	</ViewTransition>
286	```
287	
288	The CSS for directional slides:
289	
290	```css filename="app/globals.css"
291	::view-transition-old(.nav-forward) {
292	  --slide-offset: -60px;
293	  animation:
294	    150ms ease-in both fade reverse,
295	    400ms ease-in-out both slide reverse;
296	}
297	::view-transition-new(.nav-forward) {
298	  --slide-offset: 60px;
299	  animation:
300	    210ms ease-out 150ms both fade,
301	    400ms ease-in-out both slide;
302	}
303	
304	::view-transition-old(.nav-back) {
305	  --slide-offset: 60px;
306	  animation:
307	    150ms ease-in both fade reverse,
308	    400ms ease-in-out both slide reverse;
309	}
310	::view-transition-new(.nav-back) {
311	  --slide-offset: -60px;
312	  animation:
313	    210ms ease-out 150ms both fade,
314	    400ms ease-in-out both slide;
315	}
316	
317	@keyframes slide {
318	  from {
319	    translate: var(--slide-offset);
320	  }
321	  to {
322	    translate: 0;
323	  }
324	}
325	```
326	
327	The 60px offset is enough to communicate direction without making the user track a fast-moving element across the screen.
328	
329	#### Anchoring the header
